{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014AjfWC4iutLDdG6skD8msV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewerguidelines soundness excitement overall assessment score scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"Incentives 2026 – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026\"},{\"title\":\"ARR SAC Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Changelog – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog\"},{\"title\":\"Guidelines for Ethics Reviewing\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ethicsreviewertutorial\"},{\"title\":\"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing\"}]\n\nI found relevant information from the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) website about reviewer guidelines regarding soundness, excitement, and overall assessment scores. Here are the key details:\n\n## Three-Score System\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Soundness Score\n\nSoundness evaluates whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them, with consideration for experimental depth/breadth and methodological validity, the adequacy of field representation in position papers and surveys, and data collection methodology for resource papers.\n\nThe soundness scale includes ratings from 5 (Excellent) to lower scores, with 5 = Excellent being \"one of the most thorough I have seen,\" 4 = Strong providing \"sufficient support for all of its claims,\" and 3 = Acceptable providing \"sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n\n## Excitement Score\n\nExcitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field. The scale includes 5 = Highly Exciting (recommending the paper to others or attending its presentation) and 4 = Exciting (mentioning the paper to others or making an effort to attend).\n\n## Overall Assessment Score\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f100d3-931f-4e74-a45a-f78f1352b916","uuid":"6663118f-4494-44d1-8dc4-6b3a4d110de8","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:50:26.967Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewerguidelines soundness excitement overall assessment score scale","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017MrNQWMETAxdCJ2hBfMK3D","content":[{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"Incentives 2026 – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026"},{"title":"ARR SAC Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Changelog – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog"},{"title":"Guidelines for Ethics Reviewing","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ethicsreviewertutorial"},{"title":"How ARR works – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewing"}]},"I found relevant information from the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) website about reviewer guidelines regarding soundness, excitement, and overall assessment scores. Here are the key details:\n\n## Three-Score System\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n## Soundness Score\n\nSoundness evaluates whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims and provides adequate support for them, with consideration for experimental depth/breadth and methodological validity, the adequacy of field representation in position papers and surveys, and data collection methodology for resource papers.\n\nThe soundness scale includes ratings from 5 (Excellent) to lower scores, with 5 = Excellent being \"one of the most thorough I have seen,\" 4 = Strong providing \"sufficient support for all of its claims,\" and 3 = Acceptable providing \"sufficient support for its main claims.\"\n\n## Excitement Score\n\nExcitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field. The scale includes 5 = Highly Exciting (recommending the paper to others or attending its presentation) and 4 = Exciting (mentioning the paper to others or making an effort to attend).\n\n## Overall Assessment Score\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue. This is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":7.540283204000007}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B6UcRJcpBYkugsoYWgLX8R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me also get the exact ARR review form with the specific scale definitions. Let me search for that.\n\nActually, I have enough information from the search results. The ARR uses a 1-5 scale for three dimensions:\n- Soundness (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=..., 1=...)\n- Excitement (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=..., 2=..., 1=...)\n\n\n- Overall Assessment (5=..., 4=..., 3=..., 2=..., 1=...)\n\nNow I'll set up the review process by dispatching two reviewers—one focused on methods and experiments, another on novelty and related work—and then run them through the full roundtable discussion with independent reviews, cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis steps.\n\nLooking at the paper, it's a large-scale empirical study examining how correctness probes transfer across languages and architectures, covering three major models, 20-50 languages, and five different transfer conditions. The core contributions are the depth-transferability Pareto frontier that varies by architecture, evidence that capability differences account for most of the transfer degradation, a CaCLR method that improves zero-shot performance by 3.5 percentage points in AUROC, and an interesting finding about Simpson's Paradox in the relationship between CKA similarity and transfer success.\n\nNow I'm checking which experiments from the planned studies are actually covered in the paper. The authors have implemented the full Pareto frontier analysis for both Qwen3-235B and Llama 4 Scout, the CKA cross-lingual similarity analysis with Simpson's Paradox, the CaCLR method with comparative evaluation, difficulty-controlled ablations to decompose the transfer degradation, linear projection sensitivity analysis, probing control tasks, correctness-confidence orthogonality analysis, and probe complexity comparisons.\n\nI'm also seeing coverage for cross-benchmark generalization, quantization validation ablations, and evaluations across three different architectures including Gemma 4 31B Dense, plus extended language coverage. All 13 planned experiments appear to be included in the paper, so the experimental scope is comprehensive.\n\nNow I'm setting up the parallel review process by dispatching two independent reviewers—one focused on methods and experiments, another on novelty and related work—to begin their initial assessments simultaneously.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1784,"cache_read_input_tokens":69326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1784},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f100d3-931f-4e74-a45a-f78f1352b916","uuid":"79d800a0-3416-4848-b3ff-2155cb67ed15"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B6UcRJcpBYkugsoYWgLX8R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Full paper read (14 pages + appendix). No explicit venue stated — using ACL ARR 1-5 scale (Soundness, Excitement, Overall Assessment). Now dispatching two independent reviewers in parallel for Step 1."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1784,"cache_read_input_tokens":69326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1784},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f100d3-931f-4e74-a45a-f78f1352b916","uuid":"22a6e2e5-c8f6-4b2c-88a7-b0833b77c309"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01B6UcRJcpBYkugsoYWgLX8R","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PxKzPQrpkGLF9FTrSKfKxu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL ARR peer review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Produce an independent, detailed review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Geometric Analysis Reveals When and Why Cross-Lingual Calibration Transfer Succeeds\"\n\nThe paper presents the first large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer. English logistic regression probes trained on internal LLM representations are applied zero-shot to other languages. Three architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense), 20–50 languages, 5 transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot, CaCLR).\n\n**Four claims forming a causal chain:**\n1. **Pareto discovery**: Architecture-dependent depth-transferability tradeoff. Deeper layers → better English probes but worse cross-lingual transfer. Qwen3: strong (ρ=-0.91, 15/20 significant), Llama 4: weak/mixed (ρ=-0.78, 6/20 significant, 3 reversed). Pareto knee at 55–58% depth.\n2. **Decomposition**: Correctness = difficulty-shared (65% var, 12% degradation) + capability-specific (35% var, 88% degradation). Capability drives deep-layer failure.\n3. **CaCLR method**: L_CaCLR = L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency (MMD on 1-D logits). +3.5pp AUROC over zero-shot, gains concentrated at deep layers (+4.8pp). No target labels needed, only parallel activations.\n4. **Simpson's Paradox in CKA–transfer**: Raw pooled CKA-AUROC correlation is negative (depth confound). Partial correlation controlling depth is significantly positive on both architectures (Qwen: +0.48, p=0.002; Llama: +0.41, p=0.008; Gemma: +0.44, p=0.004). Within-layer CKA genuinely predicts transfer. Correctness subspace angle: 12.4° within-layer (near-universal), 38.7° cross-layer.\n\n**Key experimental details:**\n- Models: Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPTQ-Int4, 94 layers, 24 probed), Llama 4 Scout (W4A16, 48 layers, 12 probed), Gemma 4 31B (FP16, 60 layers, 15 probed)\n- Languages: 20 core (HIGH: zh/es/fr/de/ja/nl/pt/it; MID: ar/hi/ko/tr/ru/vi/th; LOW: sw/bn/yo/ig/te), extended to 50\n- Benchmark: GlobalMMLU (test split 70/30 for train/eval)\n- Probe: Logistic regression with StandardScaler, also tested MLP\n- CaCLR: Adam lr=1e-3, batch 256, 50 epochs, patience 10, β optimal at 0.05, α=0.1\n- Quantization validation: ≤0.5pp mean diff, rank correlation ≥0.994\n- Control tasks: Hewitt & Liang (2019) selectivity, 27% low-resource artifact rate\n- Cross-benchmark: Belebele, MMLU-ProX (CaCLR improvement consistent, CV=0.04)\n- Linear projection: ~42 pairs to 95% performance; Procrustes reversal at ZS AUROC ~0.52\n- Probe complexity: MLP gains +2pp EN but loses 5.5pp in transfer\n\n**Tables in paper:** 24 tables across main text and appendix.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the **methodological soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Cover:\n\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed to support the claims? Any missing controls or baselines?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are significance tests appropriate? Sample sizes adequate? Bootstrap CIs?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to reproduce? Hyperparameter choices justified?\n4. **Potential confounds**: Quantization effects? Benchmark-specific artifacts? Language selection bias?\n5. **CaCLR method**: Is the method well-motivated? Fair comparison with baselines? Ablation sufficient?\n6. **Decomposition**: Is the difficulty-capability decomposition sound? Random-direction control sufficient?\n7. **CKA analysis**: Is the Simpson's Paradox analysis convincing? Partial correlation methodology correct?\n8. **Coverage of experiment plan**: All 13 planned experiments are covered in the paper.\n\n## Scoring (ACL ARR 1–5)\n- **Soundness**: 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Borderline, 1=Poor\n- **Overall Assessment**: 5=Top-tier, 4=Accept, 3.5=Findings-worthy, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Output Format\nProduce your review in this structure:\n```\n### R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Soundness Score**: X/5\n**Overall Assessment**: X/5\n\n#### Summary of Contributions (2-3 sentences)\n\n#### Strengths (numbered list)\n\n#### Weaknesses (numbered list)\n\n#### Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n\n#### Minor Issues (numbered list)\n\n#### Missing Experiments or Controls\n\n#### Detailed Assessment of Each Claim\n- Claim 1 (Pareto): ...\n- Claim 2 (Decomposition): ...\n- Claim 3 (CaCLR): ...\n- Claim 4 (CKA/Simpson): ...\n```\n","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1784,"cache_read_input_tokens":69326,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1784},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e2f100d3-931f-4e74-a45a-f78f1352b916","uuid":"91d5ac6b-1c4a-41c8-8035-0f0fb56b0d56"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a6ef8079015e30cb5","tool_use_id":"toolu_01PxKzPQrpkGLF9FTrSKfKxu","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an ACL ARR peer review. Your focus is **methods and experiments**. Produce an independent, detailed review.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"Geometric Analysis Reveals When and Why Cross-Lingual Calibration Transfer Succeeds\"\n\nThe paper presents the first large-scale study of cross-lingual correctness probe transfer. English logistic regression probes trained on internal LLM representations are applied zero-shot to other languages. Three architectures (Qwen3-235B MoE, Llama 4 Scout MoE, Gemma 4 31B Dense), 20–50 languages, 5 transfer conditions (zero-shot, mean-shift, linear projection, few-shot, CaCLR).\n\n**Four claims forming a causal chain:**\n1. **Pareto discovery**: Architecture-dependent depth-transferability tradeoff. Deeper layers → better English probes but worse cross-lingual transfer. Qwen3: strong (ρ=-0.91, 15/20 significant), Llama 4: weak/mixed (ρ=-0.78, 6/20 significant, 3 reversed). Pareto knee at 55–58% depth.\n2. **Decomposition**: Correctness = difficulty-shared (65% var, 12% degradation) + capability-specific (35% var, 88% degradation). Capability drives deep-layer failure.\n3. **CaCLR method**: L_CaCLR = L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency (MMD on 1-D logits). +3.5pp AUROC over zero-shot, gains concentrated at deep layers (+4.8pp). No target labels needed, only parallel activations.\n4. **Simpson's Paradox in CKA–transfer**: Raw pooled CKA-AUROC correlation is negative (depth confound). Partial correlation controlling depth is significantly positive on both architectures (Qwen: +0.48, p=0.002; Llama: +0.41, p=0.008; Gemma: +0.44, p=0.004). Within-layer CKA genuinely predicts transfer. Correctness subspace angle: 12.4° within-layer (near-universal), 38.7° cross-layer.\n\n**Key experimental details:**\n- Models: Qwen3-235B-A22B (GPTQ-Int4, 94 layers, 24 probed), Llama 4 Scout (W4A16, 48 layers, 12 probed), Gemma 4 31B (FP16, 60 layers, 15 probed)\n- Languages: 20 core (HIGH: zh/es/fr/de/ja/nl/pt/it; MID: ar/hi/ko/tr/ru/vi/th; LOW: sw/bn/yo/ig/te), extended to 50\n- Benchmark: GlobalMMLU (test split 70/30 for train/eval)\n- Probe: Logistic regression with StandardScaler, also tested MLP\n- CaCLR: Adam lr=1e-3, batch 256, 50 epochs, patience 10, β optimal at 0.05, α=0.1\n- Quantization validation: ≤0.5pp mean diff, rank correlation ≥0.994\n- Control tasks: Hewitt & Liang (2019) selectivity, 27% low-resource artifact rate\n- Cross-benchmark: Belebele, MMLU-ProX (CaCLR improvement consistent, CV=0.04)\n- Linear projection: ~42 pairs to 95% performance; Procrustes reversal at ZS AUROC ~0.52\n- Probe complexity: MLP gains +2pp EN but loses 5.5pp in transfer\n\n**Tables in paper:** 24 tables across main text and appendix.\n\n## Your Task\nEvaluate the **methodological soundness** and **experimental rigor**. Cover:\n\n1. **Experimental design**: Are the experiments well-designed to support the claims? Any missing controls or baselines?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are significance tests appropriate? Sample sizes adequate? Bootstrap CIs?\n3. **Reproducibility**: Enough detail to reproduce? Hyperparameter choices justified?\n4. **Potential confounds**: Quantization effects? Benchmark-specific artifacts? Language selection bias?\n5. **CaCLR method**: Is the method well-motivated? Fair comparison with baselines? Ablation sufficient?\n6. **Decomposition**: Is the difficulty-capability decomposition sound? Random-direction control sufficient?\n7. **CKA analysis**: Is the Simpson's Paradox analysis convincing? Partial correlation methodology correct?\n8. **Coverage of experiment plan**: All 13 planned experiments are covered in the paper.\n\n## Scoring (ACL ARR 1–5)\n- **Soundness**: 5=Excellent (most thorough), 4=Strong (sufficient for all claims), 3=Acceptable (sufficient for main claims), 2=Borderline, 1=Poor\n- **Overall Assessment**: 5=Top-tier, 4=Accept, 3.5=Findings-worthy, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n\n## Output Format\nProduce your review in this structure:\n```\n### R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n**Soundness Score**: X/5\n**Overall Assessment**: X/5\n\n#### Summary of Contributions (2-3 sentences)\n\n#### Strengths (numbered list)\n\n#### Weaknesses (numbered list)\n\n#### Questions for Authors (numbered list)\n\n#### Minor Issues (numbered list)\n\n#### Missing Experiments or Controls\n\n#### Detailed Assessment of Each Claim\n- Claim 1 (Pareto): ...\n- Claim 2 (Decomposition): ...\n- Claim 3 (CaCLR): ...\n- Claim 4 (CKA/Simpson): ...\n```\n","uuid":"5d3ce76e-5a3d-461f-897d-2ff97f1e34db","session_id":"e2f100d3-931f-4e74-a45a-f78f1352b916"}
