{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DTJg3iRjKp9Sqz69PH6yEm","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0519_2/projects/xling_calib_direction/writing_plan.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"meta:\\\\n  venue: EACL 2027\\\\n  track: long\\\\n  template: docs/paper/  # ACL style files (acl.sty, acl_natbib.bst) from github.com/acl-org/acl-style-files\\\\n  template_note: \\\\\\\"Downloaded 2026-05-21 from official acl-org/acl-style-files repo.\\\\\\\"\\\\n  page_limit: 8\\\\n  deadline: 2026-08-03\\\\n  core_claim: >-\\\\n    Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer reveals an architecture-dependent\\\\n    depth-transferability tradeoff; the natural distributional-matching approach\\\\n    (CaCLR) fails, but linear projection with ~50 parallel pairs is the practical\\\\n    solution. A Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer provides geometric insight into\\\\n    when and why transfer succeeds.\\\\n  created_at: 2026-05-21T08:00:00Z\\\\n  terminology_fixes:\\\\n    - old: \\\\\\\"zero-label\\\\\\\"\\\\n      new: \\\\\\\"zero-correctness-label\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - old: \\\\\\\"First systematic study\\\\\\\"\\\\n      new: \\\\\\\"First large-scale study comparing correctness probe transfer across architectures\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\npaper_outline:\\\\n  - section: abstract\\\\n    word_budget: 220\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Problem: Non-English languages suffer 5x worse calibration; all probe-based calibration methods are English-only\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Approach: First large-scale study — 2+ architectures × 20 languages × 5 transfer methods on GlobalMMLU\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 1: Architecture-dependent Pareto frontier (Qwen strong tradeoff, Llama language-specific)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 2: Simpson's Paradox in CKA-transfer — raw negative, partial positive (geometric insight)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 3: ~28% low-resource probing results are capacity artifacts (control task warning)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 4: Linear projection ~50 pairs achieves 98% of full performance; Procrustes reversal at AUROC>0.60\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Finding 5: CaCLR distributional matching fails — geometric analysis explains why\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method, introduction]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: introduction\\\\n    word_budget: 1000\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Hook: Non-English LLM users face 5x worse calibration (ECE gap, cite LACE), yet all probe-based calibration methods operate English-only\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Gap: Fixes require per-language labels (LACE) or 5x inference cost (MKA); nobody has tested correctness probe transfer multilingually. Xiao et al. probed knowledge boundaries (input-level answerability), not correctness (output-dependent accuracy)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Insight: Correctness direction occupies language-agnostic low-dimensional subspace, but transfer governed by architecture-specific depth dynamics and a Simpson's Paradox in geometric alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Approach: Systematic comparison of 5 transfer methods across architectures and 20 languages; CaCLR tests distributional matching; difficulty-capability decomposition explains failure modes\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Contributions: (1) architecture-dependent Pareto discovery + CKA geometric analysis with Simpson's Paradox, (2) 28% control-task artifact warning for the field, (3) linear projection practical recipe (~50 pairs, resource-tier stratification), (4) CaCLR principled negative result + decomposition analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method]\\\\n    figures: [fig1_pareto]\\\\n    citation_hints:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Zhou et al. LACE — 5x ECE gap across 100+ languages\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Miao & Ungar 2026 — correctness-confidence orthogonality\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Xiao et al. ACL 2025 — closest prior work, differentiate knowledge boundary vs correctness\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"CORAL, CCPS, Confidence Manifold — all English-only\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: related_work\\\\n    word_budget: 1000\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 1 — Mechanistic calibration: Miao & Ungar (orthogonality), CORAL (cross-task probes), CCPS (perturbation stability), Confidence Manifold (low-dim structure), Joshi (layer evolution), Ji (verbal uncertainty direction) → all English-only, single architecture; we extend to multilingual multi-architecture\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 2 — Cross-lingual probing & alignment: Xiao (knowledge boundary ≠ correctness), Civelli (difficulty ≠ correctness), DALI (causal activation patching), Liu & Niehues (middle-layer alignment), Pires/Wu & Dredze (multilingual BERT transfer) → we add depth analysis, Pareto frontier, 5 transfer methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 3 — Multilingual calibration: LACE (per-language ensemble), temperature scaling → require per-language labels; we require zero correctness labels\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 4 — Theoretical: Ben-David (H-divergence), Zhao et al. 2019 (invariant representations + conditional shift) → we empirically test distributional matching prediction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cluster 5 — Methodology: Hewitt & Liang (control tasks), Voita & Titov (MDL), CKA (Kornblith), MUSE/VecMap (projection baselines), conformal prediction (complementary) → we apply controls to cross-lingual setting; projection motivated by MUSE/VecMap\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Table 1: side-by-side differentiation from 5 closest works (Xiao, LACE, CORAL, Miao & Ungar, Civelli)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [introduction]\\\\n    tables: [tab1_related_work]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: method\\\\n    word_budget: 1200\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"3.1 Problem formulation: correctness probing (definition: p(correct|h_l)), notation, contrast with knowledge boundary (Xiao) and difficulty (Civelli)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"3.2 Experimental framework: models (Qwen3-8B/Llama4-Scout + MVP models), 20 languages by resource tier (HIGH/MED/LOW), GlobalMMLU test split 70/30 (probe_train/eval), activation extraction (register_forward_hook, post-norm, INT8/FP16)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"3.3 Transfer methods: (a) zero-shot (direct application), (b) mean-shift + rank-invariance proof (1-2 para formal argument why ΔAUROC≡0), (c) Procrustes alignment (parallel pairs, effect reversal analysis), (d) few-shot (25/50/100 target labels), (e) CaCLR (L_BCE + α·L_SB-ECE + β·L_consistency; MMD on 1-D probe logits from parallel GlobalMMLU items; implementation spec for reproducibility)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"3.4 Difficulty-capability decomposition: IRT difficulty probes → project out shared component → retrain on residual → prescriptive predictions\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"3.5 Geometric analysis: CKA across layers controlling for depth (partial correlation), subspace angle measurement, Simpson's Paradox characterization\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"3.6 Probing controls: Hewitt & Liang selectivity, permuted-label baseline, PCA dimensionality reduction (plan_013 protocol)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: []\\\\n    figures: [fig2_method_pipeline]\\\\n    registry_refs: [mvp_001, mvp_003, plan_013]\\\\n    notation_table:\\\\n      h_l: \\\\\\\"hidden representation at layer l\\\\\\\"\\\\n      w_l: \\\\\\\"learned probe weight vector (correctness direction) at layer l\\\\\\\"\\\\n      AUROC: \\\\\\\"Area Under ROC for correctness prediction\\\\\\\"\\\\n      ECE: \\\\\\\"Expected Calibration Error (15-bin)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      CKA: \\\\\\\"Centered Kernel Alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n      SB-ECE: \\\\\\\"Soft-Binned ECE (differentiable, Karandikar 2021)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - section: experiments\\\\n    word_budget: 1800\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.1 Setup: 2+ architectures × 20 languages × 12-24 layers × 5 transfer methods; metrics (AUROC, ECE, Brier, selectivity); bootstrap CIs (1000 resamples) + paired permutation tests; Cohen's d effect sizes\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.2 Main transfer results (Table 2): zero-shot → mean-shift → Procrustes → few-shot → CaCLR, grouped by architecture and resource tier [TODO plan_001, plan_002, plan_003]\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.3 Pareto frontier (Figure 1): per-architecture depth-transferability scatter; per-language Spearman rho quantifying tradeoff strength; architecture comparison\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.4 CKA geometric analysis (Figure 3): Simpson's Paradox — raw rho=-0.40 (p<0.01, Llama pilot) vs partial rho=+0.41 (p=0.006); transparency: Qwen pilot non-significant (rho=+0.18, p=0.274); bootstrap CIs; subspace angles\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.5 Decomposition (Figure 4): difficulty-shared vs capability-specific transfer by resource tier and depth [TODO plan_004]\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.6 Empirical findings (Table 3): (a) control task: 28% low-resource artifacts (selectivity<threshold at L20-L27), (b) projection: 50 pairs → 98%, HIGH saturate ~22, LOW ~48, (c) Procrustes reversal at zero-shot AUROC>0.60\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"4.7 CaCLR analysis: β sweep across 3 OOM, component ablation, comparison with linear projection, negative result + geometric explanation (MMD matches marginals but not low-dim directional structure) [TODO plan_003]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [method]\\\\n    tables: [tab2_main_results, tab3_empirical]\\\\n    figures: [fig1_pareto, fig3_cka_simpson, fig4_decomposition, fig5_layer_auroc]\\\\n    registry_refs: [mvp_001, mvp_002, mvp_003, plan_013]\\\\n\\\\n  - section: conclusion\\\\n    word_budget: 350\\\\n    core_bullets:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Summary: field-level viability test for multilingual probe calibration — architecture-dependent Pareto, CKA geometric insight, practical 50-pair recipe\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Limitations: MCQA only (GlobalMMLU), 2-3 architectures (observational, no causal attribution to specific features), CaCLR 1-D MMD design limitation, INT8 quantization\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Field warning: 28% capacity artifacts → all cross-lingual probing studies should include selectivity checks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Future: multilingual probe pooling on high-resource languages, adapter-based alignment, extension to open-ended generation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    dependencies: [experiments, method, introduction]\\\\n\\\\nvisualization_plan:\\\\n  - fig_id: fig1_pareto\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Hero figure: Pareto scatter — English probe AUROC (x) vs mean cross-lingual transfer AUROC (y) across layers, separate trajectories per architecture\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Deeper layers improve English probes but degrade cross-lingual transfer in\\\\n      Qwen (strong Pareto tradeoff); Llama shows language-specific patterns where\\\\n      some languages improve with depth — the tradeoff shape is architecture-dependent.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern A: background bands for layer depth zones (shallow L0-8, mid L12-20, deep L24+) — show where tradeoff kicks in\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Connect layers with thin lines to show trajectory (path through Pareto space)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Different marker shapes per architecture (circle=Qwen, triangle=Llama)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern F: per-label offset for key layer annotations (L0, L16, L24, L27/L31)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Dashed Pareto frontier line for Qwen to highlight envelope\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Reference: calibration literature Pareto scatter (see CORAL Fig 3 style)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: mvp_001, mvp_002, mvp_003 [TODO: plan_001, plan_002 for full-scale 20 langs]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: introduction\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig2_method_pipeline\\\\n    type: conceptual\\\\n    tool: paperbanana\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Method overview: LLM → layer activations → English probe → 5 transfer paths → multilingual evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Our framework systematically tests 5 transfer strategies from English-trained\\\\n      correctness probes to target languages, ranging from zero-shot to CaCLR\\\\n      distributional matching, evaluated across the full layer depth.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Left-to-right flow: multilingual LLM → extract h_l → train English probe → branch into 5 transfer methods\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"5 methods as parallel paths: zero-shot (direct), mean-shift, Procrustes, few-shot, CaCLR\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"CaCLR path highlighted: show BCE + SB-ECE + MMD(parallel) components\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Bottom: evaluation across 20 languages × resource tiers\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Keep minimal — max 3 vertical levels, no decorative elements\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"conceptual\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: method\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"16:9\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig3_cka_simpson\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Simpson's Paradox dual panel: (left) raw CKA vs transfer with negative trend, (right) per-layer partial correlations showing positive trend\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      Raw CKA-transfer correlation is negative (depth confound — deeper layers have\\\\n      lower CKA AND lower transfer), but within each layer, higher cross-lingual CKA\\\\n      predicts better transfer — a Simpson's Paradox revealing genuine geometric structure.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern H: multi-panel asymmetric (1.4:1), left=aggregated, right=layer-stratified\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Left: scatter colored by depth (viridis gradient), global regression line (negative slope), annotate rho_raw and p-value\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Right: separate mini regression lines per layer (same depth colors), show positive slopes, annotate rho_partial\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern J: path-effect white stroke on annotations for readability\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Panel titles as insights: left='Aggregate: depth confounds CKA-transfer', right='Per-layer: CKA predicts transfer'\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Transparency: if Qwen non-significant, mark with hollow markers + note\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: mvp_003 CKA data [TODO: plan_001, plan_002 for full-scale + plan_011 partial correlation]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"2:1\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig4_decomposition\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Difficulty-capability decomposition: transfer AUROC curves for full probe vs difficulty-only vs capability-residual across layers\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      The difficulty-shared component transfers well at all depths (flat curve);\\\\n      the capability-specific component drives deep-layer degradation, explaining\\\\n      why transfer fails for some languages at later layers.\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Three lines per resource tier: full transfer (solid), difficulty-only (dashed), capability-residual (dotted)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern B: fill_between for gap between full and difficulty-only (shows capability contribution)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern A: vertical band marking 'degradation zone' where capability component diverges\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Facet by resource tier (HIGH/MED/LOW) or overlay with alpha differentiation\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"X-axis: normalized layer depth (0-1); Y-axis: transfer AUROC\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"[TODO: plan_004 decomposition experiments]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"4:3\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - fig_id: fig5_layer_auroc\\\\n    type: statistical\\\\n    tool: matplotlib\\\\n    description: \\\\\\\"Layer-wise AUROC: English vs cross-lingual transfer across all layers, side-by-side panels per architecture\\\\\\\"\\\\n    narrative_intent: >-\\\\n      English AUROC increases monotonically with depth in both architectures, but\\\\n      cross-lingual transfer peaks at intermediate layers — the divergence point and\\\\n      pattern differ by architecture (Qwen: sharp drop after L24; Llama: gradual, language-dependent).\\\\n    design_notes:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern H: side-by-side panels (Qwen | Llama), shared y-axis\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"English line bold blue; target languages by resource tier color (HIGH=green, MED=orange, LOW=red)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern C: horizontal dashed line at English best AUROC as reference\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Pattern A: vertical band marking 'optimal transfer zone' per architecture\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Thin lines per language, thick line for tier mean — show both individual variation and trend\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Reference: layer analysis line plots universal in this subfield (distilled-writing)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: mvp_001, mvp_002, mvp_003 [TODO: plan_001, plan_002 full-scale]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    aspect_ratio: \\\\\\\"2:1\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\ntable_plan:\\\\n  - table_id: tab1_related_work\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"Comparison with closely related work on calibration probing and cross-lingual transfer\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: related_work\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns: [Paper, Target Signal, \\\\\\\"# Langs\\\\\\\", Metrics, \\\\\\\"Labels Req.\\\\\\\", Depth Analysis, Transfer Methods]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"manual\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Rows: Xiao et al. (knowledge boundary), LACE (per-language ensemble), CORAL (cross-task English),\\\\n      Miao & Ungar (orthogonality), Civelli (difficulty), Ours. Checkmarks for binary features.\\\\n      Highlight our unique combination: correctness + 20 langs + 0 correctness labels + full depth + 5 methods.\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab2_main_results\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"Cross-lingual correctness probe transfer results (AUROC) across 5 methods, grouped by resource tier\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: comparison\\\\n    columns: [Method, \\\\\\\"HIGH (5L avg)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"MED (5L avg)\\\\\\\", \\\\\\\"LOW (5L avg)\\\\\\\", Overall, \\\\\\\"Best Layer\\\\\\\"]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: [TODO plan_001, plan_002, plan_003]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Rows: zero-shot, mean-shift, Procrustes, few-shot-25, few-shot-50, few-shot-100, CaCLR.\\\\n      Separate sub-tables per architecture. Bold best per column. Include ECE in parentheses.\\\\n      MVP data (mvp_001, mvp_003) available for preliminary version.\\\\n    baseline_row: \\\\\\\"zero-shot\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - table_id: tab3_empirical\\\\n    caption: \\\\\\\"Key empirical findings with practical implications\\\\\\\"\\\\n    placement: experiments\\\\n    type: statistics\\\\n    columns: [Finding, Evidence, Threshold/Value, Implication]\\\\n    data_source: \\\\\\\"registry: plan_013, mvp_001, mvp_003 [TODO: plan_012]\\\\\\\"\\\\n    notes: >-\\\\n      Three rows: (1) Control task: 28% low-resource results below selectivity threshold → field warning,\\\\n      (2) Projection saturation: ~50 pairs (HIGH ~22, LOW ~48) → practical recipe,\\\\n      (3) Procrustes reversal: helps when AUROC<0.50, hurts when >0.60 → conditional strategy.\\\\n\\\\nliterature_strategy:\\\\n  citation_budget: 35\\\\n  macro_context:\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"LLM calibration and confidence estimation via internal representations\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"cross-lingual representation transfer and alignment in multilingual LLMs\\\\\\\"\\\\n    - \\\\\\\"probing methodology, controls, and representation similarity\\\\\\\"\\\\n  micro_clusters:\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"mechanistic_calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"correctness direction probing language models 2025 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"calibration internal representations LLM residual stream\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"confidence manifold neural network representations\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Miao & Ungar 2026 (correctness-confidence orthogonality)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Miao 2026 CORAL (cross-task correctness probes)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Khanmohammadi 2025 CCPS (perturbation stability)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Confidence Manifold 2026 (low-dimensional structure)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Joshi 2025 (calibration across layers)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Ji 2025 (verbal uncertainty linear feature)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 8\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"cross_lingual_probing\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"cross-lingual probe transfer multilingual LLM 2024 2025 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"knowledge boundary probing across languages\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"cross-lingual difficulty probing shared geometry multilingual\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Xiao et al. ACL 2025 (knowledge boundary)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Civelli 2026 (shared geometry of difficulty)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 5\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"cross_lingual_alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"cross-lingual representation alignment multilingual LLM 2024 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"activation patching cross-lingual transfer\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"MUSE VecMap cross-lingual embedding alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Ravisankar 2026 DALI\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Liu & Niehues 2025 (middle-layer alignment)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Conneau et al. 2018 MUSE\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Artetxe et al. 2018 VecMap\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Pires et al. 2019 (multilingual BERT)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Wu & Dredze 2019 (cross-lingual transfer)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 7\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"multilingual_calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"multilingual LLM calibration ECE cross-lingual 2024 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"temperature scaling multilingual language models\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Zhou et al. LACE (adaptive layer ensemble)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 4\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"domain_adaptation_theory\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"domain adaptation theory H-divergence representation learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"invariant representations domain adaptation conditional shift Zhao 2019\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Ben-David et al. 2010 (H-divergence)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Zhao et al. 2019 (invariant representations + conditional failure)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 3\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"probing_methodology\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"probing classifier control task selectivity Hewitt Liang\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"MDL probes Voita Titov online coding\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"probing methodology neural network representations 2019 2020\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Hewitt & Liang 2019 (control tasks)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Voita & Titov 2020 (MDL probes)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 3\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"representation_similarity\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"CKA centered kernel alignment representation similarity\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"SVCCA representation comparison neural networks\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Kornblith et al. 2019 (CKA)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 2\\\\n\\\\n    - cluster_name: \\\\\\\"calibration_foundations\\\\\\\"\\\\n      search_queries:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"neural network calibration temperature scaling Guo 2017\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"conformal prediction language models coverage guarantee\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"soft binned ECE differentiable calibration\\\\\\\"\\\\n      must_cite:\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Guo et al. 2017 (modern calibration)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (conformal prediction)\\\\\\\"\\\\n        - \\\\\\\"Karandikar 2021 (SB-ECE)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      target_count: 3\\\\n\\\\nwriting_order:\\\\n  - method          # Can start now — framework clear from paper_framing\\\\n  - related_work    # Can start now — literature-based, addresses reviewer concern\\\\n  - experiments     # Needs P0 data; setup section writable now, results after plan_001/002\\\\n  - introduction    # Needs method + experiments framing\\\\n  - abstract        # Last — distills all sections\\\\n  - conclusion      # Last — mirrors abstract + limitations\\\\n\\\\nappendix_plan:\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"A: Per-language detailed results (20 languages × all layers × 5 methods)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"B: CaCLR hyperparameter sensitivity (α and β sweeps)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"C: INT8 vs FP16 validation ablation\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"D: Full CKA matrices and subspace angle tables\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"E: Mean-shift rank-invariance formal proof\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"F: Additional Pareto plots (per-language individual trajectories)\\\\\\\"\\\\n  - \\\\\\\"G: Procrustes sensitivity analysis (pairs vs ΔAUROC curves)\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 21359, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PxKzPQrpkGLF9FTrSKfKxu","session_id":"e2f100d3-931f-4e74-a45a-f78f1352b916","uuid":"840a0873-66da-4ff1-b124-5ac2477449f3","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:51:46.803Z"}
